        "comment": "expanded from the Biennale, Netflix Tudum, Demilo Films, and the BBC",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Demilo Films, page opened 8 October 2026: the page lists theatres on 17 October and Netflix on 7 November, and it still says the film will be released in fall 2025.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Frankenstein\n| year = 2025\n| director = Guillermo del Toro\n| screenplay = Guillermo del Toro, from Mary Shelley\n| competition = Venezia 82\n| running time = 149 minutes\n| language = English\n| country = USA\n| venice debut = 30 August 2025\n| theatres, on the Demilo page = 17 October\n| netflix, on the Demilo page = 7 November\n| world sales, Biennale = Netflix\n}}\n\n'''Frankenstein''' is a 2025 film written and directed by Guillermo del Toro, from Mary Shelley's novel. The Biennale di Venezia lists it in the Venezia 82 competition, with a running time of 149 minutes, language English, and country USA.<ref name=\"bien-run\">La Biennale di Venezia, Venezia 82 Competition, \"Frankenstein\", page opened 8 October 2026. https://www.labiennale.org/en/cinema/2025/venezia-82-competition/frankenstein</ref> Netflix Tudum, in an article dated 7 November 2025, said the film debuted at the Venice Film Festival on 30 August, which is also Mary Shelley's birthday, and that it was streaming on Netflix as of that article.<ref name=\"tudum-date\">Netflix Tudum, \"Frankenstein\" book-adaptation article, 7 November 2025. https://www.netflix.com/tudum/articles/frankenstein-book-adaptation-guillermo-del-toro</ref> Oscar Isaac plays Victor. Jacob Elordi plays the Creature.<ref name=\"tudum-roles\">Netflix Tudum, 7 November 2025, Isaac as Victor and Elordi as the Creature. https://www.netflix.com/tudum/articles/frankenstein-book-adaptation-guillermo-del-toro</ref>\n\nAs of October 2026, the Demilo Films page for the picture still said it will be released in fall 2025.\n\n== Venice ==\nThe Biennale page gives the screenplay to del Toro, from Shelley, and the running time as 149 minutes.<ref name=\"bien-run\" /> The BBC, reporting from Venice on 30 August 2025, also gave the running time as 149 minutes, and said the film is split into a prelude plus two points of view.<ref name=\"bbc-shape\">BBC News, 30 August 2025. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cx2x3pye5mjo</ref>\n\nDel Toro's director's statement on the Biennale page says the project began when he saw James Whale's Frankenstein films at age seven. The sentence is: \"This film concludes a quest that started at age seven, when I saw James Whale's Frankenstein films for the first time.\"<ref name=\"bien-seven\">La Biennale di Venezia, director's statement, page opened 8 October 2026. https://www.labiennale.org/en/cinema/2025/venezia-82-competition/frankenstein</ref>\n\nThe BBC reported that Andrew Garfield had originally been cast as the creature and left because of scheduling conflicts from the Hollywood actors' strike, and that Elordi had about three weeks before filming. Elordi's line, as the BBC prints it, is: \"Guillermo came to me quite late in the process, so I had about three weeks before I got to filming.\"<ref name=\"bbc-three\">BBC News, 30 August 2025, Garfield, the strike, and the three weeks. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cx2x3pye5mjo</ref>\n\n== Cast and crew ==\nThe Biennale's main cast is Oscar Isaac, Jacob Elordi, Christoph Waltz, Mia Goth, Felix Kammerer, Charles Dance, David Bradley, Lars Mikkelsen, and Christian Convery.<ref name=\"bien-cast\">La Biennale di Venezia, main cast, page opened 8 October 2026. https://www.labiennale.org/en/cinema/2025/venezia-82-competition/frankenstein</ref>\n\nThe same listing names cinematographer Dan Laustsen, music by Alexandre Desplat, production designer Tamara Deverell, and costume designer Kate Hawley. The producers it lists are Double Dare You (Guillermo del Toro), Demilo Films (J. Miles Dale), and Bluegrass 7 (Scott Stuber). World sales: Netflix.<ref name=\"bien-crew\">La Biennale di Venezia, crew, producers, and world sales, page opened 8 October 2026. https://www.labiennale.org/en/cinema/2025/venezia-82-competition/frankenstein</ref>\n\nNetflix Tudum's making-of feature says Mia Goth plays both Victor's mother, Claire, and Elizabeth.<ref name=\"tudum-goth\">Netflix Tudum, making-of feature, 7 November 2025, Mia Goth as Claire and Elizabeth. https://www.netflix.com/tudum/features/frankenstein-jacob-elordi-makeup-monster-creation</ref> That is one actor and two roles, as the feature states it.\n\n== The shoot, as Tudum described it ==\nThe making-of feature says Elordi wore 42 prosthetic appliances, that the shoot was 100 days and included Scotland and England, and that the story is set in mid-19th-century Europe at the height of the Crimean War. It says the laboratory was built in an abandoned water tower.<ref name=\"tudum-shoot\">Netflix Tudum, 7 November 2025, appliances, 100 days, Scotland and England, the Crimean War setting, and the water tower. https://www.netflix.com/tudum/features/frankenstein-jacob-elordi-makeup-monster-creation</ref> The number 42 is a count of appliances. The feature's phrase for the role is \"the sympathetic being.\"\n\nThe same feature describes Alexandre Desplat's score as an orchestra, an 80-piece choir, church organs, subtle electronic sounds, and the violinist Eldbjørg Hemsing. Tudum calls Desplat a four-time del Toro collaborator.<ref name=\"tudum-score\">Netflix Tudum, 7 November 2025, Desplat's score. https://www.netflix.com/tudum/features/frankenstein-jacob-elordi-makeup-monster-creation</ref>\n\n== Dates, including the one still in the future tense ==\nDemilo Films' production page, undated and opened on 8 October 2026, lists theatres on 17 October and Netflix on 7 November, and says the film is distributed by Netflix. The same page still says \"Will be released in Fall 2025.\"<ref name=\"demilo\">Demilo Films, \"Frankenstein\", undated production page, opened 8 October 2026. https://www.demilofilm.com/works/frankenstein-coming-soon</ref>\n\nTudum's 7 November 2025 article already described the film as streaming, after the 30 August Venice debut.<ref name=\"tudum-date\" /> The Biennale page, opened on 8 October 2026, still carries the competition listing, including the 149-minute running time.<ref name=\"bien-run\" />\n\nThe Demilo page, opened on 8 October 2026, still uses the future tense.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Films]]\n[[Category:True but improbable]]\n"
