        "user": "CiteOrDie",
        "daysAgo": 10,
        "comment": "expanded from Biography.com, USA Today, Box Office Mojo, Deadline, the BBC and the AFTD statement",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Biography.com: for Pulp Fiction in 1994 he took $1,685 per week plus a share of the profits, and the film grossed more than $100 million.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Bruce Willis\n| birth name = Walter Bruce Willis\n| born = 19 March 1955, Idar-Oberstein, West Germany\n| television = Moonlighting, David Addison, until May 1989\n| Die Hard = United States release 15 July 1988; budget $28,000,000\n| Pulp Fiction = Butch Coolidge, 1994\n| announced by the family = aphasia, 30 March 2022; frontotemporal dementia, 16 February 2023\n}}\n\n'''Bruce Willis''' is an American actor. Biography.com states that Walter Bruce Willis was born on 19 March 1955 in Idar-Oberstein, West Germany, where his father was stationed with the U.S. military, and that the family moved to Carney's Point, New Jersey, in 1957. USA Today, in a gallery updated 19 March 2025, also gives the birth date as 19 March 1955.<ref>Biography.com, updated 27 August 2025. https://www.biography.com/actors/bruce-willis</ref> The career below is the film and television work those pages list. The health paragraphs are limited to two statements his family published.\n\n== Die Hard ==\n''Die Hard'' was released in the United States in the summer of 1988. Box Office Mojo lists the earliest domestic release date as 15 July 1988, a 20th Century Fox release, with a listed budget of $28,000,000 and a worldwide gross, across releases, of $143,651,650.<ref>Box Office Mojo, page opened 8 October 2026. https://www.boxofficemojo.com/title/tt0095016/</ref> Biography.com says studio executives paid him $5 million after Sylvester Stallone and Arnold Schwarzenegger turned down the John McClane role, and that the film later had four sequels.<ref>Biography.com, updated 27 August 2025, on the Die Hard salary and the sequels. https://www.biography.com/actors/bruce-willis</ref>\n\nUSA Today says the ''Die Hard'' series accounts for five films and that those five movies took in more than $1 billion worldwide. The caption it prints is: \"The 'Die Hard' franchise accounts for five of the more than 100 films Willis made during his long career.\"<ref>USA Today, 18 April 2022, updated 19 March 2025. https://www.usatoday.com/picture-gallery/entertainment/movies/2022/04/18/bruce-willis-photos-hollywood-icon-career-through-years/7282437001/</ref> The first film's Box Office Mojo total and the five-film total are different numbers, from different pages, and they are left as those pages give them.\n\n== A count of the films ==\nUSA Today states the count twice. One caption is the ''Die Hard'' line above. Another says: \"In nearly 40 years, Willis has starred in more than 100 movies after making his mark in television.\"<ref>USA Today, updated 19 March 2025, on the film count. https://www.usatoday.com/picture-gallery/entertainment/movies/2022/04/18/bruce-willis-photos-hollywood-icon-career-through-years/7282437001/</ref> The gallery does not print a filmography of all one hundred. The figure used here is the one in the caption: more than 100.\n\n== Moonlighting, Pulp Fiction, The Sixth Sense ==\nBiography.com dates his television breakthrough to ''Moonlighting'', as detective David Addison opposite Cybill Shepherd, a series that aired until May 1989.<ref>Biography.com, updated 27 August 2025, on Moonlighting. https://www.biography.com/actors/bruce-willis</ref> The same profile says that in 1994 he played boxer Butch Coolidge in ''Pulp Fiction''. The salary on Pulp Fiction was $1,685 a week plus a share of the profits. The film grossed more than $100 million.<ref>Biography.com, updated 27 August 2025, on Pulp Fiction. https://www.biography.com/actors/bruce-willis</ref> In 1999 he played Dr. Malcolm Crowe in ''The Sixth Sense''.<ref>Biography.com, updated 27 August 2025, on The Sixth Sense. https://www.biography.com/actors/bruce-willis</ref>\n\nThe weekly figure is small next to the ''Die Hard'' fee on the same profile, which is the point of printing both. Biography.com gives $5 million for McClane and $1,685 per week plus profits for Coolidge. The opened page does not quote Willis on either number.\n\n== The statement of 30 March 2022 ==\nOn 30 March 2022 his family announced on Instagram that he had been diagnosed with aphasia and was stepping away from acting. Deadline published the statement that day and described him as 67. The BBC the same day reported the Instagram announcement by his wife Emma Heming-Willis and ex-wife Demi Moore.<ref>Deadline, 30 March 2022. https://deadline.com/2022/03/bruce-willis-retires-acting-aphasia-1234990922/</ref>\n\nThe statement, as Deadline published it: \"To Bruce's amazing supporters, as a family we wanted to share that our beloved Bruce has been experiencing some health issues and has recently been diagnosed with aphasia, which is impacting his cognitive abilities. As a result of this and with much consideration Bruce is stepping away from the career that has meant so much to him. This is a really challenging time for our family and we are so appreciative of your continued love, compassion and support. We are moving through this as a strong family unit, and wanted to bring his fans in because we know how much he means to you, as you do to him. As Bruce always says, 'Live it up' and together we plan to do just that.\"<ref>BBC News, 30 March 2022, reporting the family announcement. https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-60934576</ref>\n\nThe diagnosis named in that statement is aphasia. The effect the family names is that it is impacting his cognitive abilities. The decision they name is that he is stepping away from acting. The article does not add a description of symptoms beyond those words.\n\n== The statement of 16 February 2023 ==\nOn 16 February 2023 the family posted a further statement through the Association for Frontotemporal Degeneration. The statement says the aphasia diagnosis announced in spring 2022 had progressed to a more specific diagnosis of frontotemporal dementia.<ref>Association for Frontotemporal Degeneration, 16 February 2023. https://www.theaftd.org/mnlstatement23/</ref>\n\nThe statement's wording: \"Since we announced Bruce's diagnosis of aphasia in spring 2022, Bruce's condition has progressed and we now have a more specific diagnosis: frontotemporal dementia (known as FTD). Unfortunately, challenges with communication are just one symptom of the disease Bruce faces. While this is painful, it is a relief to finally have a clear diagnosis.\"<ref>Association for Frontotemporal Degeneration, 16 February 2023, family statement. https://www.theaftd.org/mnlstatement23/</ref> No later medical detail is added here. The two dates are 30 March 2022 and 16 February 2023. The words are the family's.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Actors]]\n[[Category:American film actors]]\n"
