        "comment": "expanded from the England squad profile, UEFA.com and Real Madrid",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=England Football squad profile, opened 8 October 2026: 60 senior caps and 14 senior goals. UEFA.com, statistics up to 4 June 2026: 51 international appearances and 8 goals.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Jude Bellingham\n| born = 29 June 2003, Stourbridge, England\n| height = 6 ft 1 in (186 cm)\n| club = Real Madrid\n| shirt = 5\n| previous clubs = Birmingham City; Borussia Dortmund\n| England debut = 12 November 2020\n| England caps = 60 senior caps, 14 senior goals, page opened 8 October 2026\n| legacy number = 1258\n| UEFA tally to 4 June 2026 = 51 appearances, 8 goals\n}}\n\n'''Jude Bellingham''' is an England international midfielder at Real Madrid. The England Football senior squad profile lists him as born 29 June 2003 in Stourbridge, England, height 6 ft 1 in (186 cm), a product of Birmingham City, with grassroots club Stourbridge Juniors and County FA Birmingham.<ref name=\"eng\">England Football, senior squad profile, opened 8 October 2026. https://www.englandfootball.com/england/mens-senior-team/squad/jude-bellingham</ref> The profile does not give Birmingham as his birthplace. It says he is Stourbridge-born.<ref name=\"engborn\">England Football, squad profile, opened 8 October 2026, birth and academy lines. https://www.englandfootball.com/england/mens-senior-team/squad/jude-bellingham</ref>\n\n== Birmingham, then Dortmund ==\nThe England page says he made his Birmingham first-team bow in August 2019 while still 16. Borussia Dortmund \"won the race for his signature in July 2020.\" His England debut is listed as 12 November 2020, when he was 17.<ref name=\"eng\" /> The same page says he was 17 when he received his first call-up to the England senior squad in November 2020, joining the squad from the MU21s.<ref name=\"engcall\">England Football, squad profile, opened 8 October 2026, senior call-up. https://www.englandfootball.com/england/mens-senior-team/squad/jude-bellingham</ref> The quoted line on the profile is his: \"To be given the opportunity to play for your country is massive.\"<ref name=\"eng\" />\n\n== June 2023 ==\nUEFA.com says he moved to Real Madrid in June 2023. In his first season in Spain he scored what UEFA calls a career-best 19 goals as Madrid won La Liga, and he assisted in a 2-0 win over Dortmund in the Champions League final at Wembley.<ref name=\"uefa\">UEFA.com, 1 July 2026; the page says its appearance figures are statistics up to 4 June 2026. https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0282-1840da0e7cce-fb60f6c0286a-1000--jude-bellingham-records-and-stats-how-brilliant-is-the-real/</ref> The final opponent was his former club. The contribution UEFA records for that match is an assist, in a 2-0 scoreline.<ref name=\"uefa\" />\n\nCarlo Ancelotti, quoted by UEFA.com as ex-Real Madrid coach, spoke about Bellingham's maturity at 20. \"This maturity that he has, he makes us forget that he's 20 years old. He's learned very quickly, and he's very happy to be here and is enjoying his time, but always with his feet on the ground and very serious and professional.\"<ref name=\"ancelotti\">UEFA.com, 1 July 2026, quoting Carlo Ancelotti. https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0282-1840da0e7cce-fb60f6c0286a-1000--jude-bellingham-records-and-stats-how-brilliant-is-the-real/</ref> Bellingham, interviewed for Real Madrid's site on 6 May 2024, credited his coach with moving him higher than the deeper role he had played at Dortmund. \"All credit to the coach for giving me that licence. It's not easy to look at someone who has played in a deeper position for so long at Dortmund and find something that makes you think I'd do well playing a bit higher up.\"<ref name=\"rm\">Real Madrid, 6 May 2024. https://www.realmadrid.com/en-US/news/football/first-team/latest-news/bellingham-uefa-06-05-2024</ref>\n\n== Number 5 ==\nReal Madrid's site records that he wears number 5, which he links to Zinedine Zidane's shirt.<ref name=\"rm5\">Real Madrid, 6 May 2024, shirt number. https://www.realmadrid.com/en-US/news/football/first-team/latest-news/bellingham-uefa-06-05-2024</ref> The number on the club page is 5. The player he names on that point is Zidane.<ref name=\"rm5\" />\n\n== Two published tallies ==\nAs opened on 8 October 2026, the England Football profile lists 60 senior caps and 14 senior goals, legacy number 1258, and says he played for England at the 2026 World Cup, \"the youngest European player to play in four major international tournaments.\" The England profile page itself does not print an updated-on date.<ref name=\"engcaps\">England Football, squad profile, opened 8 October 2026, caps, goals, legacy number and 2026 World Cup line. https://www.englandfootball.com/england/mens-senior-team/squad/jude-bellingham</ref> UEFA.com's earlier tally, cut off at 4 June 2026, was 51 international appearances and 8 goals.<ref name=\"uefa\" /> The page that carries the 51 and the 8 is dated 1 July 2026 and labels the figures as statistics up to 4 June 2026.<ref name=\"uefatally\">UEFA.com, 1 July 2026, international tally. https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0282-1840da0e7cce-fb60f6c0286a-1000--jude-bellingham-records-and-stats-how-brilliant-is-the-real/</ref>\n\nThe England page opened on 8 October 2026 printed 60 and 14. The UEFA page, stopped at 4 June 2026, printed 51 and 8.<ref name=\"engcaps\" /><ref name=\"uefatally\" />\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Footballers]]\n[[Category:England men's internationals]]\n[[Category:Living people]]\n"
