        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Associated Press, via ESPN, 28 June 2023: the Spurs' official height for the No. 1 pick was 7 feet 3.5 inches without shoes. The NBA draft guide had listed 7 ft 4 in, and that listing was not an official measurement.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Victor Wembanyama\n| born = 4 January 2004\n| country = France\n| last attended = Metropolitans 92\n| draft = 2023, round 1, pick 1, San Antonio Spurs\n| listed height = 7 ft 4 in (2.24 m), NBA.com player page\n| shoes-off height = 7 ft 3.5 in, Spurs figure reported 28 June 2023\n| listed weight = 235 lb (107 kg)\n| 2025-26 award = Kia NBA Defensive Player of the Year\n}}\n\n'''Victor Wembanyama''' is a centre for the San Antonio Spurs. NBA.com's player page lists him as born 4 January 2004, country France, last attended Metropolitans 92, drafted 2023, round 1, pick 1, by the San Antonio Spurs.<ref name=\"nba\">NBA.com player page, opened 8 October 2026. https://www.nba.com/stats/player/1641705</ref> The page displays height 7 ft 4 in (2.24 m), weight 235 lb (107 kg), and season figures of 25.0 points, 11.5 rebounds and 3.1 assists per game. It lists 3 years of experience and age 22. The draft line on the page reads \"DRAFT 2023 R1 Pick 1.\"<ref name=\"nbadraft\">NBA.com player page, opened 8 October 2026, draft and season lines. https://www.nba.com/stats/player/1641705</ref>\n\n== The measurement ==\nThe Associated Press, published by ESPN on 28 June 2023, reported that the Spurs measured the No. 1 pick at 7 feet 3.5 inches without shoes, under NBA guidelines. The draft guide had listed him at 7 ft 4 in. The Associated Press added that the listing \"was not based off any official measurement.\" He was not measured at the Chicago combine because his French team's season was still going.<ref name=\"espn\">Associated Press via ESPN, 28 June 2023. https://www.espn.com/nba/story/_/id/37929130/victor-wembanyama-official-height-7-3-spurs-say</ref> The figure the Spurs called official is the one taken without shoes. The 7 ft 4 in on the later player page is the figure that page displays.<ref name=\"nba\" /><ref name=\"espn\" />\n\n== The 46-game line ==\nFIBA reported that the Spurs said Wembanyama would miss the rest of that NBA season after a deep-vein thrombosis, a blood clot, was found in his right shoulder after the NBA All-Star Game. FIBA's page does not give the calendar date of the Thursday announcement. The line FIBA published for that interrupted season is 46 games, not a 2025–26 injury.<ref name=\"fiba\">FIBA Basketball, \"Season over for Victor Wembanyama,\" page opened 8 October 2026. https://www.fiba.basketball/en/news/season-over-for-victor-wembanyama</ref> \"The 21-year-old, who was named 2024 NBA Rookie of the Year and made his first All-Star appearance this year, was averaging 24.3 points, 11.0 rebounds, 3.7 assists, and a league-leading 3.8 blocks in 46 games for the 23-29 Spurs.\"<ref name=\"fiba\" /> The averages and the 23-29 record are the figures on that page. The clot is what FIBA says the Spurs reported.<ref name=\"fibaline\">FIBA Basketball, 46-game line, page opened 8 October 2026. https://www.fiba.basketball/en/news/season-over-for-victor-wembanyama</ref>\n\n== 20 April 2026 ==\nNBA.com, carrying an Associated Press report by Tim Reynolds, announced on 20 April 2026 that Wembanyama won the 2025–26 Kia NBA Defensive Player of the Year award, the Hakeem Olajuwon Trophy. The page calls him the youngest winner, at 22, and the first unanimous winner. It says he led the NBA in blocked shots for a third consecutive season. No 2025–26 injury was on this page.<ref name=\"dpoy\">NBA.com / Associated Press, Tim Reynolds, 20 April 2026. https://www.nba.com/news/kia-nba-defensive-player-of-the-year-award-2025-26</ref> The report's summary is that he was second in the voting for the award as a rookie, was the favourite the next season until a medical condition ended that season prematurely, and then won it. The medical condition in that sentence is the earlier interruption. The 2025–26 award page does not add a new injury.<ref name=\"dpoy2\">NBA.com / Associated Press, 20 April 2026, voting history paragraph. https://www.nba.com/news/kia-nba-defensive-player-of-the-year-award-2025-26</ref>\n\nSpurs forward Keldon Johnson is quoted in the award story. \"Best player in the world.\"<ref name=\"dpoy\" /> The other finalists were Oklahoma City's Chet Holmgren and Detroit's Ausar Thompson. Wembanyama was also an MVP finalist alongside Shai Gilgeous-Alexander and Nikola Jokić. The page says he joins David Robinson and Michael Jordan as players who have won both Rookie of the Year and Defensive Player of the Year. \"At 22, he's the youngest winner of the award.\"<ref name=\"dpoy3\">NBA.com / Associated Press, 20 April 2026, finalists and the Robinson-Jordan line. https://www.nba.com/news/kia-nba-defensive-player-of-the-year-award-2025-26</ref>\n\nThe shoes-off measurement from 28 June 2023 was 7 feet 3.5 inches. The award dated 20 April 2026 was unanimous, and the page that announced it listed no 2025–26 injury.<ref name=\"espn\" /><ref name=\"dpoy\" />\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Basketball players]]\n[[Category:San Antonio Spurs]]\n[[Category:Living people]]\n"
