        "comment": "expanded from the ICC final report and the pre-tournament guide",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=ICC, 8 March 2026: Jasprit Bumrah took 4 for 15 in four overs, and the ICC says that was the first four-wicket haul in a men's T20 World Cup final.}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = 2026 Men's T20 World Cup\n| dates = 7 February 2026 to 8 March 2026\n| hosts = India and Sri Lanka\n| venues = 8\n| teams = 20\n| matches = 55\n| final = 8 March 2026, Narendra Modi Stadium, Ahmedabad\n| result = India beat New Zealand by 96 runs\n| India = 255 for 5\n| New Zealand = 159 all out in 19 overs\n| defending champions = India, winners in 2024\n}}\n\nThe '''2026 Men's T20 World Cup''' is over. The ICC's final report, dated 8 March 2026, says India beat New Zealand by 96 runs in Ahmedabad and became the first team to win back-to-back men's T20 World Cup titles.<ref name=\"final\">ICC, 8 March 2026. https://www.icc-cricket.com/tournaments/mens-t20-world-cup-2026/news/live-india-new-zealand-chase-world-cup-glory</ref> \"India defeated New Zealand by 96 runs to become the first team to win back-to-back T20WC titles.\"<ref name=\"finallead\">ICC, 8 March 2026, the result line. https://www.icc-cricket.com/tournaments/mens-t20-world-cup-2026/news/live-india-new-zealand-chase-world-cup-glory</ref>\n\n== The hosts and the dates ==\nThe ICC's pre-tournament guide, dated 6 February 2026, says India and Sri Lanka were the hosts, across eight venues: Ahmedabad (Narendra Modi Stadium), Kolkata (Eden Gardens), Delhi (Arun Jaitley Stadium), Mumbai (Wankhede Stadium), Chennai (M. A. Chidambaram Stadium), the R. Premadasa Stadium and SSC in Colombo, and Pallekele in Kandy.<ref name=\"guide\">ICC, 6 February 2026. https://www.icc-cricket.com/tournaments/mens-t20-world-cup-2026/news/your-guide-to-the-icc-men-s-t20-world-cup-2026-key-details</ref> \"India and Sri Lanka will host the tournament, with games set to be played across eight venues - five stadiums in five different Indian cities and three Sri Lankan grounds including two different Colombo venues.\"<ref name=\"guidevenues\">ICC, 6 February 2026, the venues paragraph. https://www.icc-cricket.com/tournaments/mens-t20-world-cup-2026/news/your-guide-to-the-icc-men-s-t20-world-cup-2026-key-details</ref>\n\nThat guide's key dates are: opener 7 February, Pakistan against the Netherlands in Colombo; Super 8s from 22 February; semi-finals on 4 and 5 March; the final on 8 March. It lists 20 teams, four groups of five, and 55 matches.<ref name=\"guide\" /> \"February 7: Tournament opener. February 22: Super 8 stage begins. March 4: Semi-final 1. March 5: Semi-final 2. March 8: The Final.\"<ref name=\"guidedates\">ICC, 6 February 2026, key dates. https://www.icc-cricket.com/tournaments/mens-t20-world-cup-2026/news/your-guide-to-the-icc-men-s-t20-world-cup-2026-key-details</ref> The guide lists 55 matches.\n\n== 255 for 5 ==\nIn the final, the ICC says India made 255 for 5, which it calls the highest total in a T20 World Cup final, and New Zealand were bowled out for 159 in 19 overs.<ref name=\"final\" /> \"New Zealand were blown away by the power-packed Indian batting line-up, who posted 255/5, the highest total at a T20 World Cup final. In response, the Black Caps folded at 159 in 19 overs.\"<ref name=\"finalscore\">ICC, 8 March 2026, the innings totals. https://www.icc-cricket.com/tournaments/mens-t20-world-cup-2026/news/live-india-new-zealand-chase-world-cup-glory</ref> The difference between 255 and 159 is 96. The margin the ICC prints is 96 runs.<ref name=\"final\" />\n\nThe ICC says the win made India the first team to defend the men's T20 World Cup, the first to win one at home, and the first to win three titles. \"The victory ensured India became the first team to defend their T20 World Cup title, the first to triumph at a home championship, and the first to win three T20 World Cup titles.\"<ref name=\"finalthree\">ICC, 8 March 2026, the three firsts. https://www.icc-cricket.com/tournaments/mens-t20-world-cup-2026/news/live-india-new-zealand-chase-world-cup-glory</ref>\n\n== Four wickets, and an 89 ==\nJasprit Bumrah took 4 for 15 in four overs. The ICC says that was the first four-wicket haul in a T20 World Cup final. Axar Patel took 3 for 27 in three overs. Sanju Samson scored 89 off 46 balls, with five fours and eight sixes.<ref name=\"final\" /> \"It was the first time that a bowler had claimed a four-wicket haul in a T20 World Cup final.\"<ref name=\"bumrah\">ICC, 8 March 2026, Bumrah's figures. https://www.icc-cricket.com/tournaments/mens-t20-world-cup-2026/news/live-india-new-zealand-chase-world-cup-glory</ref> The four overs and the 15 runs are the line attached to that claim. The 89, the 46 balls, the five fours and the eight sixes are Samson's line in the same report.<ref name=\"samson\">ICC, 8 March 2026, Samson and Axar Patel. https://www.icc-cricket.com/tournaments/mens-t20-world-cup-2026/news/live-india-new-zealand-chase-world-cup-glory</ref>\n\n== The title they were defending ==\nEntering the 2026 event, the ICC guide identified India as defending champions after beating South Africa by seven runs in the 2024 final in Barbados. Virat Kohli made 76 off 59 and was player of the match. Jasprit Bumrah took 15 wickets and was player of the tournament.<ref name=\"2024\">ICC, 6 February 2026, the 2024 final note. https://www.icc-cricket.com/tournaments/mens-t20-world-cup-2026/news/your-guide-to-the-icc-men-s-t20-world-cup-2026-key-details</ref> \"India beat South Africa in the 2024 Final, claiming their second Men's T20 World Cup crown, winning the decider by seven runs.\"<ref name=\"2024b\">ICC, 6 February 2026, 2024 result. https://www.icc-cricket.com/tournaments/mens-t20-world-cup-2026/news/your-guide-to-the-icc-men-s-t20-world-cup-2026-key-details</ref> The 2026 win is the one the final report calls a successful defence, a home win, and a third title.<ref name=\"finalthree\" />\n\nNew Zealand's innings lasted 19 overs. The margin was 96 runs.<ref name=\"finalscore\" />\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Cricket]]\n[[Category:T20 World Cup]]\n[[Category:2026 in cricket]]\n"
