        "daysAgo": 11,
        "comment": "expanded from Biography.com, Variety and Vulture",
        "content": "{{true and funny|source=Interview magazine, September 2013, via Biography.com: \"I was a big song-and-dance type of kid — you know, one of those kids with jazz hands.\"}}\n{{Infobox\n| title = Scarlett Johansson\n| born = 22 November 1984, Manhattan\n| parents = Karsten Johansson; Melanie Sloan\n| film debut = North (1994)\n| Black Widow = Iron Man 2 (2010) through Avengers: Endgame (2019); solo film 2021\n| Tony = Best Featured Actress in a Play, A View from the Bridge, 2010\n| 2019 nominations = Marriage Story; Jojo Rabbit\n}}\n\n'''Scarlett Johansson''' is an American actress. Biography.com states that Scarlett Ingrid Johansson was born on 22 November 1984 in Manhattan. Her father, Karsten Johansson, is a Danish architect, and her mother, Melanie Sloan, is a producer and actor. At 8 she appeared off-Broadway in ''Sophistry'' with Ethan Hawke. Her movie debut was Rob Reiner's ''North'' (1994).<ref>Biography.com, updated 3 July 2025. https://www.biography.com/actors/scarlett-johansson</ref> On being a child performer, she told ''Interview'' magazine in September 2013: \"I was a big song-and-dance type of kid — you know, one of those kids with jazz hands.\"<ref>Biography.com, updated 3 July 2025, quoting Interview magazine, September 2013. https://www.biography.com/actors/scarlett-johansson</ref>\n\n== 2003 ==\nIn ''Lost in Translation'' (2003), directed by Sofia Coppola, she played a woman visiting Tokyo who forms a relationship with an older man played by Bill Murray. The same year she played the servant girl painted by Vermeer, opposite Colin Firth, in ''Girl with a Pearl Earring''. Biography.com says both roles brought Golden Globe nominations for Best Actress.<ref>Biography.com, updated 3 July 2025, on Lost in Translation and Girl with a Pearl Earring. https://www.biography.com/actors/scarlett-johansson</ref> The opened page does not quote her on either film. The nominations are both for Best Actress, both from 2003, for two different pictures.\n\n== Black Widow ==\nShe first played Black Widow in ''Iron Man 2'' (2010), opposite Robert Downey Jr., then in ''The Avengers'' (2012) and later Marvel films through ''Avengers: Endgame'' (2019). Her solo film ''Black Widow'' was released in 2021. Biography.com says that in 2018 she was first on Forbes' list of the world's highest-paid actresses.<ref>Biography.com, updated 3 July 2025, on Black Widow and the Forbes list. https://www.biography.com/actors/scarlett-johansson</ref>\n\nVariety reported on 30 September 2021 that she and Disney had settled her lawsuit over ''Black Widow''. She had accused Disney of abandoning an exclusive theatrical release in favour of a simultaneous Disney Plus Premier Access release and of costing her box-office bonuses. Terms of the settlement were not disclosed. Variety says she had sought a $50 million payout. Disney had said she was paid $20 million and accused her of \"callous disregard\" for the pandemic. The film had been set for 1 May 2020, then opened on 9 July 2021 in theaters and on Disney Plus for a $30 rental. Variety says an August filing put streaming gross at $125 million and box office at $367 million.<ref>Variety, 30 September 2021. https://variety.com/2021/film/news/scarlett-johansson-disney-lawsuit-settled-1235078355/</ref>\n\nHer statement to Variety: \"I am happy to have resolved our differences with Disney. I'm incredibly proud of the work we've done together over the years and have greatly enjoyed my creative relationship with the team. I look forward to continuing our collaboration in years to come.\" Disney Studios Content chairman Alan Bergman: \"I'm very pleased that we have been able to come to a mutual agreement with Scarlett Johansson regarding 'Black Widow.'\"<ref>Variety, 30 September 2021, quoting Johansson and Alan Bergman. https://variety.com/2021/film/news/scarlett-johansson-disney-lawsuit-settled-1235078355/</ref>\n\nThe $50 million is the payout Variety says she sought. The $20 million is what Disney said she was paid. The settlement terms are the figure Variety does not print. The August filing's two grosses are $125 million streaming and $367 million box office. The rental price on Disney Plus was $30. The original date was 1 May 2020. The opening date was 9 July 2021.\n\n== Her ==\nIn 2013 she voiced Samantha, the operating system, in Spike Jonze's ''Her'', opposite Joaquin Phoenix. Vulture reported on 21 June 2013 that the role had been Samantha Morton's and that Johansson replaced her in post-production. Warner Bros. had set the release for 20 November.<ref>Vulture, 21 June 2013. https://www.vulture.com/2013/06/spike-jonze-replaced-samantha-morton-with-scarjo.html</ref> Biography.com also records the voice role.<ref>Biography.com, updated 3 July 2025, on Her. https://www.biography.com/actors/scarlett-johansson</ref>\n\nJonze, by email to Vulture: \"Samantha was with us on set and was amazing. It was only in post production, when we started editing, that we realized that what the character/movie needed was different from what Samantha and I had created together. So we recast and since then Scarlett has taken over that role.\"<ref>Vulture, 21 June 2013, quoting Spike Jonze by email. https://www.vulture.com/2013/06/spike-jonze-replaced-samantha-morton-with-scarjo.html</ref> The email says Morton was on set, and that the recast happened in post-production. The release date Vulture gives, as Warner Bros. had set it, is 20 November.\n\n== The Tony and the double nomination ==\nIn 2010 she made her Broadway debut as Catherine in a revival of Arthur Miller's ''A View from the Bridge'', opposite Liev Schreiber, and won the Tony Award for Best Featured Actress in a Play.<ref>Biography.com, updated 3 July 2025, on the Tony Award. https://www.biography.com/actors/scarlett-johansson</ref>\n\nAt the 2019 Oscars cycle she was nominated twice in the same year: Best Actress for ''Marriage Story'', opposite Adam Driver, and Best Supporting Actress for ''Jojo Rabbit'', as the mother of a boy in Nazi Germany. Biography.com says that made her the 12th performer nominated in both categories in one year.<ref>Biography.com, updated 3 July 2025, on the 2019 nominations. https://www.biography.com/actors/scarlett-johansson</ref> On ''Marriage Story'', she told The Hollywood Reporter: \"By the time we had made the film, I was in a more settled place. But obviously I had my own fresh perspective on the story.\"<ref>Biography.com, updated 3 July 2025, quoting her to The Hollywood Reporter on Marriage Story. https://www.biography.com/actors/scarlett-johansson</ref>\n\nThe Broadway prize is 2010, the same year as ''Iron Man 2''. The two Oscar nominations are 2019, the year of ''Avengers: Endgame'' on the Biography.com list. The count Biography.com gives is the 12th performer nominated in both acting categories in one year. The quote is about being in a more settled place by the time the film was made. The article does not expand that sentence into a private life the page does not describe.\n\n== References ==\n{{reflist}}\n\n[[Category:Actors]]\n[[Category:American film actors]]\n"
